From 8cb184145bdf8d5ec5354230ca4e4cffce0cba41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 03:04:51 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] t/constellation: pre-delivery trusted-gravity gate --
 close T221's trigger-blindness loop

T221 (2026-08-19, first worn run of the 0082 stack) measured trySeededRecovery
firing ZERO times across a whole ghost-flood session: the tracker always found
a wrong-lobe pose that passed reprojection scoring, delivered it, and the WMR
driver's device-side gravity gate then discarded 93-99.7% of them -- from where
the recovery trigger sits, every ghost looked like a success, so the rescue
ladder never ran. Compounding it, every yaw-gated layer (0076's trusted
orientation, 0077's seeding) stayed inert because the ghost flood kept
solve_yaw_locked from ever establishing -- while the gravity information that
could have rejected those ghosts needs no yaw lock at all.

Fix, option (a) of the T222 design pass: a new OPTIONAL tracking-source
callback, get_trusted_gravity, gated only on IMU data flowing (deliberately
NOT on solve_yaw_locked -- that asymmetry is the point), returning the fusion's
world-down in the delivered-pose convention (the exact post-Rx180 down_imu the
device-side gate computes). The tracker checks candidates against it BEFORE
committing (deviceGravityRejected, called at all three pushPose sites:
tryDevicePose, tryDeviceBlobRecovery, processSampleSlow) -- a wrong-lobe
candidate becomes an ordinary "not found", so the existing recovery ladder
including seeded recovery triggers naturally, no changes to the fast-path loop.
Expected cascade: only true-lobe samples reach the solve-yaw correction loop,
so the lock can finally form under motion, waking the yaw-gated layers.

Opt-in via WMR_CONSTELLATION_TRACKER_GRAVITY_GATE_DEG (default 0/off; suggested
14, same as the device-side gate, which stays on as the last line of defense
against post-refinement drift). rift/pssense: byte-identical behavior -- the
callback is optional and neither sets it, same proven pattern as 0076's
get_trusted_orientation.

Signed-off-by: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
---
